A1. A post at Princeton. ~ GOAL
A4. Some letters that need answering. ~ SOS
A7. This is a fake. ~ SHAM
A10. A shot in the face. ~ NIP
A11. Sticky stuff. ~ LAC
A12. Cracks about this are dangerous. ~ ICE
A13. Foundation of the American home (pl.). ~ LOTS
A15. Four-wheeled rumble seat. ~ LORRY
A16. You said it! ~ ORAL
A18. Playing doubles. ~ DUETS
A20. A Greek hick. ~ PSI
A21. Dry humor. ~ DROLL
A22. This should be seen but not heard. ~ SOUP
A24. Here's where the plumbers get a break. ~ MAIN
A27. And here's a liner a shortstop can't handle. ~ OLYMPIC
A30. Sons o' guns. ~ CADETS
A33. A good comeback. ~ REBATE
A37. This lad said "The moving finger writes; and having writ, moves on." ~ OMAR
A38. These are shed by many a chorus girl. ~ TEARS
A39. A minor matter. ~ ASIA
A40. This little pig went to market. ~ BIGTOE
A42. The head man. ~ HATTER
A44. The kind of measures angry butchers resort to. ~ DRASTIC
A48. Silence! ~ HIST
A49. Balloon-sized pome. ~ EPIC
A52. A rummy fellow. ~ DEMON
A54. What they wear on their weak ends in Scotland. ~ TAM
A56. The lad with the high hat. ~ DUNCE
A59. The sign that made an impression on the sign painter. ~ OMEN
A60. Sillies. ~ SIMPS
A61. This kind of horse is easily spotted. ~ PIED
A62. If you're a poor fish this may puzzle you. ~ NET
A63. Dunk. ~ RET
A64. What the lad they said would amount to nothing actually amounted to. ~ NIL
A65. A very contrary current. ~ EDDY
A66. Once upon a time there was a printer—— ~ ENS
A67. College ogre. ~ DEAN

D1. An expensive mouthful. ~ GOLD
D2. Dora's poker relative. ~ ANTE
D3. These were long at Christmas. ~ LISTS
D4. Bum dressing. ~ SLOPPY
D5. You're certainly all at sea without these. ~ OARS
D6. How to grow a bankroll (provided you have something to start with). ~ SCRIMP
D7. What did Lucretia Borgia hold her lovers in? ~ SCORN
D8. The kid himself. ~ HERO
D9. Manhattan sparking place. ~ MALL
D14. The most interesting child in the world. ~ OUR
D17. Give me lots of this and I'll be hoppy. ~ ALE
D19. This may give you a dirty look. ~ SOOT
D21. Ride these and you'll ride to the poorhouse. ~ DICE
D23. Homes for moths. ~ ULSTERS
D25. This reminded Rover of a sausage. ~ AIRSHIP
D26. A wiseguy thought this up. ~ ADAGE
D28. European trips are a cinch with these. ~ MEANS
D29. A popular waster. ~ HASTE
D30. All that was left of the vegetarian's dinner. ~ COB
D31. A friend from France. ~ AMI
D32. The middle of the berth. ~ ERT
D34. A fly-by-night. ~ BAT
D35. Blessed is the one that doesn't wrinkle. ~ TIE
D36. A dirt collector. ~ EAR
D41. The fellow who invented the Norse Code. ~ ODIN
D43. This'll make a hole in one. ~ ACID
D45. She went to Paris to change this. ~ ATTIRE
D46. Makes goo-goo eyes at. ~ TEMPTS
D47. This suffered a severe drop recently in Paris. ~ HEM
D48. Cleo was sweet to Mark; Juliet to Romeo; Lizzie to Henry: but who is sweet to every man? ~ HONEY
D50. The king of hearts. ~ CUPID
D51. A lofty mortal. ~ ACE
D52. Congressional vacuum. ~ DOME
D53. What to do after you go on a rip. ~ MEND
D55. That's all there is, there isn't any more. ~ AMEN
D57. The old green river. ~ NILE
D58. Residence of the very first people of the land. ~ EDEN


